/* Openings library. Each entry: [eco, family, variation, side you learn it as ('w'|'b'), moves (SAN), summary, key ideas, optional {ply: comment}] */
window.OPENINGS_RAW = [
// ---------------- 1.e4 e5 ----------------
['C60','Ruy Lopez','Closed, Main Line','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 d6 c3 O-O h3','The Spanish: one of the oldest and richest openings. White pressures the e5 pawn indirectly by attacking its defender, the c6 knight.',['Bb5 hits the defender of e5 — it is pressure, not a threat yet (Bxc6 then Nxe5 can be met by ...Qd4).','c3 and h3 prepare d4 without allowing ...Bg4 pins.','The light-squared bishop on b3 rakes f7 for the whole game.','Black aims for ...Na5, ...c5 and queenside space.'],{4:'Bb5 attacks the knight that defends e5.',5:'a6 asks the bishop a question at once.',14:'c3 prepares the d4 break.',16:'h3 stops ...Bg4 so d4 can follow.'}],
['C65','Ruy Lopez','Berlin Defense','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nf6 O-O Nxe4 d4 Nd6 Bxc6 dxc6 dxe5 Nf5 Qxd8+ Kxd8','The "Berlin Wall": Black trades queens early and reaches a famously solid endgame with the bishop pair.',['Black gives up castling rights but has no weaknesses and two bishops.','White has a kingside pawn majority; Black has doubled c-pawns but they control key squares.','Black king walks to c8/e8; Black plans ...h6, ...Be6/...Bd7 and ...b6.'],{7:'Black grabs the e4 pawn; it will be returned.',15:'Queens come off — a strategic endgame.'}],
['C68','Ruy Lopez','Exchange Variation','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Bxc6 dxc6 O-O f6 d4 exd4 Nxd4 c5 Nb3','White gives up the bishop pair to damage Black\'s pawns. In the endgame White\'s healthy 4-vs-3 kingside majority can create a passed pawn.',['White wants to trade pieces and reach a pawn endgame.','Black has the bishop pair and open lines — Black wants to keep pieces on.','...f6 defends e5 solidly.'],{5:'White trades the bishop for the knight to double Black\'s pawns.'}],
['C89','Ruy Lopez','Marshall Attack','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 O-O c3 d5 exd5 Nxd5 Nxe5 Nxe5 Rxe5 c6','Black sacrifices a pawn for a lasting attack on the white king. Very well analyzed and dangerous.',['Black gets fast development: ...Bd6, ...Qh4 and ...Bg4/...Bf5.','White must defend precisely: d4, Re1, g3 and Be3 are typical.','A great example of initiative being worth more than a pawn.'],{15:'d5! — the Marshall gambit.'}],
['C80','Ruy Lopez','Open Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Nxe4 d4 b5 Bb3 d5 dxe5 Be6','Black takes the e4 pawn and fights for active piece play, accepting a slightly loose structure.',['The e4 knight is strong but can be challenged by Nbd2 or c3/Bc2.','Black\'s d5 pawn is the backbone; ...Be6 defends it.','White has a space-gaining e5 pawn and the d4 square.']],
['C63','Ruy Lopez','Schliemann Gambit','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 f5 Nc3 fxe4 Nxe4 d5','An aggressive counter-gambit: Black strikes back with ...f5 immediately.',['Black opens the f-file early for attack.','White\'s best play is calm development; the e4 knight gets kicked around.','Risky for both sides — great for players who like sharp positions.']],
['C62','Ruy Lopez','Steinitz Defense','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 d6 d4 Bd7 Nc3 Nf6 O-O Be7','Black solidly defends e5 with ...d6. Passive, but extremely hard to break.',['Black keeps a strong point on e5.','White gains space and freer development.','Black often has to give up the center with ...exd4.']],
['C64','Ruy Lopez','Classical Defense','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Bc5 c3 Nf6 d4 Bb6','Black develops the bishop to its most active diagonal, eyeing f2.',['c3-d4 gains time on the c5 bishop.','The b6 bishop pins against the f2/g1 area.','Black sometimes plays ...Nxe4 tricks against an early d4.']],
['C61','Ruy Lopez','Bird\'s Defense','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nd4 Nxd4 exd4 O-O','Black trades knights to gain a cramping d4 pawn. Unusual and rarely played at top level.',['The d4 pawn cramps White\'s c3 knight.','White develops quickly with O-O, d3 and f4.','Black must be careful: the d4 pawn can become a target.']],
['C95','Ruy Lopez','Breyer Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 d6 c3 O-O h3 Nb8 d4 Nbd7','The knight retreats to b8 to reroute to d7, freeing the c-pawn and supporting e5. Deep and strategic.',['Rerouting a badly-placed piece is worth two tempi.','After ...Nbd7, ...Bb7, ...Re8 and ...Bf8 Black has a compact setup.','A model of flexible defense.']],
['C96','Ruy Lopez','Chigorin Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 d6 c3 O-O h3 Na5 Bc2 c5 d4 Qc7','Black chases the bishop with ...Na5 and grabs queenside space with ...c5.',['Black supports e5 with ...Qc7.','The a5 knight returns via c6 or b7 later.','White often closes the center with d5 and plays on the kingside.']],
['C92','Ruy Lopez','Zaitsev Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 d6 c3 O-O h3 Bb7 d4 Re8','Black piles up on e4 with the bishop and rook. Karpov\'s main weapon.',['Black\'s pieces hit e4 from b7 and e8.','White plays Nbd2-f1-g3 to defend e4.','Tactically sharp lines with ...exd4 and ...b4.']],
['C88','Ruy Lopez','Anti-Marshall (8.a4)','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 O-O a4 b4','White avoids the Marshall gambit by attacking the b5 pawn first.',['a4 provokes ...b4 and opens the a-file.','White keeps a small, safe edge.','Avoiding an opponent\'s best weapon is a practical skill.']],
['C54','Italian Game','Giuoco Piano (Main Line)','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Bc5 c3 Nf6 d4 exd4 cxd4 Bb4+ Bd2 Bxd2+ Nbxd2 d5','The "quiet game": White builds a big pawn center with c3 and d4. Black must strike back with ...d5.',['White gets an ideal center if Black is passive.','Black\'s ...d5 break liquidates the center.','The isolated d-pawn structures that follow are common.']],
['C50','Italian Game','Giuoco Pianissimo','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Bc5 d3 Nf6 O-O d6 c3 O-O Re1 a6 Bb3 Ba7','The modern Italian: slow maneuvering. White plays d3, c3, Re1, Nbd2-f1-g3 and only later d4.',['Hidden energy: the white bishop on b3 and knight route to g3.','Black often plays ...a6 and ...Ba7 to keep the bishop safe from d4 and Na4.','The key breaks are d4 for White and ...d5 for Black.']],
['C51','Italian Game','Evans Gambit','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Bc5 b4 Bxb4 c3 Ba5 d4 exd4 O-O','White gives a pawn to gain time for c3 and d4, opening lines against Black\'s king.',['Every white move comes with tempo.','Black should return material at the right moment.','A favorite of Morphy and Kasparov — learn it to understand initiative.'],{5:'b4!? — the Evans Gambit.'}],
['C59','Two Knights Defense','Main Line 4.Ng5 d5 5.exd5 Na5','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ c6 dxc6 bxc6 Be2 h6 Nf3 e4 Ne5','Black answers the attack on f7 with ...d5 and gives up a pawn for strong development.',['After ...Na5 the knight hits the c4 bishop.','Black\'s lead in development compensates for the pawn.','White\'s knight is chased around while Black opens lines.']],
['C57','Two Knights Defense','Fried Liver Attack','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Nxd5 Nxf7 Kxf7 Qf3+ Ke6 Nc3','White sacrifices a knight to drag the black king into the center. Black must defend very carefully.',['The king on e6 is exposed to Qf3, Nc3 and d4.','Black should avoid 5...Nxd5 and play 5...Na5 instead.','A classic lesson about f7 being Black\'s weakest square.'],{9:'Nxd5? allows the Fried Liver.',10:'Nxf7! — the sacrifice.'}],
['C57','Two Knights Defense','Traxler Counterattack','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 Bc5 Nxf7 Bxf2+ Kxf2 Nxe4+','Black ignores the threat to f7 and counter-sacrifices on f2. Wild and tactical.',['Black\'s pieces swarm the white king.','Both kings are exposed — whoever calculates better wins.','Great training for calculation.']],
['C55','Two Knights Defense','Modern Bishop Line (4.d3)','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 d3 Be7 O-O O-O Re1 d6 c3','White avoids early tactics with d3, keeping a slow Italian-style game.',['Solid and flexible; transposes to Giuoco Pianissimo ideas.','White plays Nbd2-f1-g3, h3 and maybe d4 later.','Low risk for White, low risk for Black.']],
['C56','Two Knights Defense','Max Lange / 4.d4 exd4 5.O-O Nxe4','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 d4 exd4 O-O Nxe4 Re1 d5 Bxd5 Qxd5 Nc3','Open lines and tactics: White sacrifices to attack along the e-file.',['Black must return material to finish development.','The pins on the e-file are the key tactical motif.','Accurate play leads to equality.']],
['C50','Italian Game','Hungarian Defense','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Be7 d4 d6 Nc3 Nf6','Black avoids tactics by defending f7 indirectly and playing ...Be7. Solid but passive.',['Black plans ...O-O and ...exd4 at the right moment.','White has a comfortable space advantage.','A safe choice against attacking players.']],
['C45','Scotch Game','Mieses Variation','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nxc6 bxc6 e5 Qe7 Qe2 Nd5 c4','White opens the center immediately with d4. After the knight trade, e5 kicks the f6 knight.',['White gains space with e5 and c4.','Black has doubled c-pawns but central control and open b-file.','Kasparov revived the Scotch in the 1990s.']],
['C45','Scotch Game','Classical Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 exd4 Nxd4 Bc5 Be3 Qf6 c3 Nge7','Black develops the bishop with tempo on the d4 knight and adds pressure with ...Qf6.',['Black pressures d4 three times.','White needs c3 and often Bc4 or Nc2.','Active piece play balances White\'s space.']],
['C44','Scotch Game','Scotch Gambit','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 exd4 Bc4 Bc5 c3 Nf6','White delays recapturing d4 to develop with tempo; often transposes to Italian lines.',['Fast development and a strong center are White\'s goals.','Black can accept the pawn or return it with ...d5 ideas.','Tricky for unprepared opponents.']],
['C44','Scotch Game','Göring Gambit','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 exd4 c3 dxc3 Nxc3 Bb4 Bc4 d6 O-O','White sacrifices a pawn (or two) for rapid development, like a Danish gambit with Nf3 included.',['White gets two developed pieces for the pawn.','Black should consider declining with ...d5.','Teaches the value of time in the opening.']],
['C44','Ponziani Opening','Main Line','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 c3 Nf6 d4 Nxe4 d5 Ne7 Nxe5 Ng6','An old opening where c3 prepares d4. Black must counter actively.',['White wants a big center with d4.','The c3 pawn takes the best square from the b1 knight.','Black equalizes with active counterplay against e4.']],
['C48','Four Knights Game','Spanish Variation','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Bb5 Bb4 O-O O-O d3 d6 Bg5','Symmetrical development; the game stays balanced and positional.',['Both sides develop naturally and castle.','Bg5 pins the f6 knight — a typical plan.','Breaking symmetry at the right moment is the key skill.']],
['C47','Four Knights Game','Scotch Four Knights','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 d4 exd4 Nxd4 Bb4 Nxc6 bxc6 Bd3 d5 exd5 cxd5 O-O O-O Bg5','White opens the center like the Scotch, keeping a slight initiative.',['Open lines suit the side that is better developed.','Black\'s c6-d5 pawn pair gives central control.','Pins on f6 are a recurring theme.']],
['C47','Four Knights Game','Halloween Gambit','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Nc3 Nf6 Nxe5 Nxe5 d4 Ng6 e5 Ng8','A spooky knight sacrifice: White gives a piece for a huge center and attacking chances.',['Objectively unsound, but practically dangerous.','White chases Black\'s knights back to the first rank.','Black should return material and develop.']],
['C42','Petrov Defense','Classical Main Line','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nf6 Nxe5 d6 Nf3 Nxe4 d4 d5 Bd3 Nc6 O-O Be7','Black counterattacks e4 instead of defending e5. One of the most solid defenses in chess.',['Never take on e4 immediately after Nxe5 — 3...Nxe4? 4.Qe2! is a known trap.','Symmetrical pawn structure; Black aims for solid equality.','Black\'s e4 knight is the focal point.'],{4:'Nxe5 — now Black must first kick the knight with ...d6.',5:'d6! First chase the knight, then take e4.'}],
['C42','Petrov Defense','Stafford Gambit','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nf6 Nxe5 Nc6 Nxc6 dxc6 d3 Bc5','A trappy gambit: Black gives a pawn for quick development and attacking chances on f2.',['Many traps: Bg5?? Nxe4! wins.','Objectively dubious if White plays calmly (Be2, O-O, c3).','Fun at fast time controls.']],
['C41','Philidor Defense','Hanham Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 Nf6 Nc3 Nbd7 Bc4 Be7 O-O O-O','Black holds the e5 point with pawns and pieces. Solid but cramped.',['Black keeps a strong point on e5.','Watch out for tactics on f7 (Bxf7+, Ng5).','A plan with ...c6, ...b5 and ...Bb7 gains queenside space.']],
['C41','Philidor Defense','Exchange Variation','b','e4 e5 Nf3 d6 d4 exd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Be7 Be2 O-O O-O Re8','Black gives up the center but gets free piece play and pressure against e4.',['Black has fewer central pawns but no weaknesses.','The plan is ...Re8, ...Bf8 and pressure on e4.','White has more space.']],
['C40','Latvian Gambit','Main Line','b','e4 e5 Nf3 f5 Nxe5 Qf6 d4 d6 Nc4 fxe4','A very risky gambit. Black attacks e4 at once, weakening the kingside.',['Black opens the f-file and gets central pawns.','Objectively unsound but full of tricks.','Teaches why early ...f5 weakens the e8-h5 diagonal.']],
['C40','Elephant Gambit','Main Line','b','e4 e5 Nf3 d5 exd5 e4 Qe2 Nf6','Black gives a central pawn to gain time and open lines.',['Black aims for quick piece activity.','White should develop rather than hold on to material.','Rare and risky.']],
['C40','Damiano Defense','Refutation','w','e4 e5 Nf3 f6 Nxe5 fxe5 Qh5+ g6 Qxe5+ Qe7 Qxh8','Why ...f6 is a bad way to defend e5: it weakens the king and the knight sacrifice works.',['Never defend e5 with ...f6 — it takes the knight\'s best square and opens the king.','After Qh5+ the rook on h8 falls.','Better defenses are ...Nc6 or ...d6.']],
['C39','King\'s Gambit','Accepted, Kieseritzky','w','e4 e5 f4 exf4 Nf3 g5 h4 g4 Ne5','White gives the f-pawn to open the f-file and build a big center. Romantic chess at its best.',['White hits the g-pawn chain with h4.','Black tries to keep the extra pawn with ...g5.','Huge open lines for both sides.'],{3:'f4 — offering a pawn.'}],
['C34','King\'s Gambit','Accepted, Fischer Defense','b','e4 e5 f4 exf4 Nf3 d6 d4 g5 h4 g4 Ng1','Bobby Fischer\'s "bust" of the King\'s Gambit: ...d6 takes e5 away from the knight before ...g5.',['Control the e5 square, then hold the f4 pawn with ...g5.','White\'s knight is forced back to g1.','Prophylaxis beats a direct attack.']],
['C33','King\'s Gambit','Bishop\'s Gambit','w','e4 e5 f4 exf4 Bc4 Nf6 Nc3 c6','White develops the bishop before the knight, aiming at f7.',['Qh4+ is less dangerous with the bishop already on c4.','White plans d4, Nf3 and O-O.','Black strikes back with ...d5.']],
['C36','King\'s Gambit','Modern Defense','b','e4 e5 f4 exf4 Nf3 d5 exd5 Nf6','Black returns the pawn at once to open lines and develop rapidly.',['Black gives back material for development.','A practical antidote to the King\'s Gambit.','The d5 pawn becomes a target.']],
['C30','King\'s Gambit','Declined, Classical','b','e4 e5 f4 Bc5 Nf3 d6 Nc3 Nf6','Black declines the gambit with a strong bishop on c5 that stops White from castling.',['The c5 bishop pins along the g1-a7 diagonal.','White can\'t castle short easily.','A safe, principled way to meet the King\'s Gambit.']],
['C31','King\'s Gambit','Falkbeer Countergambit','b','e4 e5 f4 d5 exd5 e4 d3 Nf6','Black counter-sacrifices in the center, cramping White\'s development.',['The e4 pawn wedges into White\'s position.','White returns material to free the game.','Counter-attack is often the best defense.']],
['C29','Vienna Game','Vienna Gambit','w','e4 e5 Nc3 Nf6 f4 d5 fxe5 Nxe4 Nf3 Be7','A King\'s Gambit with Nc3 included first. White gains space on the kingside.',['White\'s e5 pawn cramps Black.','Black\'s e4 knight is strong but can be challenged.','Plans: d3, Qe2, Be2 and O-O.']],
['C27','Vienna Game','Frankenstein-Dracula','b','e4 e5 Nc3 Nf6 Bc4 Nxe4 Qh5 Nd6 Bb3 Nc6 Nb5 g6 Qf3 f5 Qd5 Qe7 Nxc7+ Kd8 Nxa8 b6','A wild line where Black sacrifices a rook to trap the a8 knight and attack.',['Black gives the exchange to dominate the center.','The a8 knight is trapped and will be won back.','Pure tactics — calculate!']],
['C25','Vienna Game','Max Lange Defense','b','e4 e5 Nc3 Nc6 Bc4 Bc5 Qg4 Qf6','A sound response to the Vienna: mirror development with the bishop on c5.',['Watch the f7 and g7 squares against early queen raids.','Black develops quickly and castles.','Symmetry with a slight tension.']],
['C24','Bishop\'s Opening','Berlin Defense','w','e4 e5 Bc4 Nf6 d3 c6 Nf3 d5 Bb3','White brings the bishop out first, keeping options of f4 or Nf3 transpositions.',['Flexible: can transpose to Italian or Vienna setups.','Black\'s ...c6 and ...d5 gain central space.','The b3 bishop keeps aiming at f7.']],
['C22','Center Game','Main Line','w','e4 e5 d4 exd4 Qxd4 Nc6 Qe3 Nf6','White opens the center at once, but the queen comes out early and is attacked by ...Nc6.',['Example of why early queen moves cost time.','White often castles long and attacks.','Black develops with tempo.']],
['C21','Danish Gambit','Accepted','w','e4 e5 d4 exd4 c3 dxc3 Bc4 cxb2 Bxb2 d5','White sacrifices two pawns for two bishops aiming at Black\'s king.',['The two bishops on c4 and b2 are monstrous.','Black should return material with ...d5.','A brilliant lesson in development over material.']],
// ---------------- Sicilian ----------------
['B90','Sicilian Defense','Najdorf, English Attack','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 a6 Be3 e5 Nb3 Be6 f3 Be7 Qd2 O-O O-O-O','The Najdorf: Fischer\'s and Kasparov\'s favorite. Black plays ...a6 to keep flexible and control b5.',['Opposite-side castling: both sides race to attack the enemy king.','Black\'s d5 square is a long-term weak spot — Black must play ...d5 when possible.','...a6 prevents Nb5 and Bb5 and prepares ...b5.'],{9:'a6 — the Najdorf move.'}],
['B96','Sicilian Defense','Najdorf, 6.Bg5','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 a6 Bg5 e6 f4 Be7 Qf3 Qc7 O-O-O Nbd7','White\'s most aggressive try: pin the f6 knight and prepare e5 or f5.',['White aims for a direct central break with e5.','Black\'s king often stays in the center for a while.','Super sharp — great to learn tactics.']],
['B92','Sicilian Defense','Najdorf, Opocensky (6.Be2)','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 a6 Be2 e5 Nb3 Be7 O-O O-O','A quieter line where White aims for positional pressure on d5.',['The fight for the d5 square defines the middlegame.','Black is solid with ...e5 and ...Be6.','White plays Be3, a4 and Nd5.']],
['B70','Sicilian Defense','Dragon, Yugoslav Attack','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 Be3 Bg7 f3 O-O Qd2 Nc6 Bc4 Bd7 O-O-O','The Dragon: Black fianchettoes the bishop on the long diagonal. The Yugoslav Attack is a race of pawn storms.',['White: Bh6 to trade the Dragon bishop, h4-h5 to open the h-file.','Black: ...Rc8, ...Ne5-c4 and the exchange sacrifice on c3.','Every tempo counts — opposite-side castling.']],
['B35','Sicilian Defense','Accelerated Dragon','b','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 g6 Nc3 Bg7 Be3 Nf6 Bc4 O-O','Black fianchettoes before ...d6, hoping to play ...d5 in one move.',['Black saves a tempo if ...d7-d5 can be played directly.','The g7 bishop exerts huge pressure on the long diagonal.','White should consider the Maroczy Bind with c4.']],
['B38','Sicilian Defense','Maroczy Bind','w','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 g6 c4 Bg7 Be3 Nf6 Nc3 O-O Be2 d6','White plants pawns on c4 and e4 to stop Black\'s freeing breaks ...b5 and ...d5.',['Space advantage: Black is cramped.','Black plays for ...a5, ...Nd7-c5 or the ...f5 break.','A great example of a positional bind.']],
['B84','Sicilian Defense','Scheveningen','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Be2 Be7 O-O O-O f4 Nc6','Black sets up a "small center" with pawns on d6 and e6. Flexible and resilient.',['The d6-e6 pawns control key squares d5, e5, f5.','Watch the Keres Attack (g4!) in other move orders.','Black counterattacks on the queenside.']],
['B67','Sicilian Defense','Richter-Rauzer','b','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Nc6 Bg5 e6 Qd2 Be7 O-O-O O-O','White pins the f6 knight to prepare Bxf6 and pressure d6.',['White often castles long and pushes f4.','Black\'s d6 pawn can become weak.','Black counterattacks with ...a6, ...b5.']],
['B33','Sicilian Defense','Sveshnikov','b','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 e5 Ndb5 d6 Bg5 a6 Na3 b5 Bxf6 gxf6 Nd5 f5','Black accepts a hole on d5 and a backward d-pawn in exchange for active pieces and the bishop pair.',['Dynamic imbalance: structure vs activity.','White\'s knight on d5 is strong; Black aims for ...f5 and kingside play.','Magnus Carlsen used it in world championship matches.']],
['B32','Sicilian Defense','Kalashnikov','b','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 e5 Nb5 d6 c4 Be7','Similar to the Sveshnikov but Black keeps ...Nf6 flexible.',['Black kicks the d4 knight right away with ...e5.','The d5 hole is the main strategic issue.','White often clamps with c4.']],
['B46','Sicilian Defense','Taimanov','b','e4 c5 Nf3 e6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nc6 Nc3 Qc7 Be3 a6 Be2 Nf6 O-O','Flexible development: Black keeps options of ...d6, ...b5 and ...Bb4.',['The queen on c7 controls e5.','Black plays on the queenside with ...b5.','A good choice for strategic players.']],
['B41','Sicilian Defense','Kan (Paulsen)','b','e4 c5 Nf3 e6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 a6 Bd3 Nf6 O-O Qc7','Black plays ...a6 early to keep the b5 square under control and stay flexible.',['Flexible pawn structure with many options.','Black\'s bishop can go to c5, b4 or e7.','White often plays c4 to clamp the center.']],
['B45','Sicilian Defense','Four Knights','b','e4 c5 Nf3 e6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 Nc6 Nxc6 bxc6 e5 Nd5','Natural development leading to sharp play after e5.',['Black\'s c6-d5 structure controls the center.','White\'s e5 pawn gains space but can become a target.','Watch for ...Qc7 and ...f6 breaks.']],
['B22','Sicilian Defense','Alapin, 2...Nf6','w','e4 c5 c3 Nf6 e5 Nd5 d4 cxd4 Nf3 Nc6 cxd4 d6','White prepares d4 with c3 to build an ideal center, avoiding the main Sicilian theory.',['White keeps a solid pawn center.','Black attacks the center with pieces (...Nd5, ...d6).','Often leads to isolated queen pawn positions.']],
['B22','Sicilian Defense','Alapin, 2...d5','w','e4 c5 c3 d5 exd5 Qxd5 d4 Nf6 Nf3 e6','Black hits back in the center immediately, putting the queen on d5 because c3 takes Nc3 away.',['The queen on d5 is safe because White has no Nc3.','White has a slight space advantage.','IQP positions are common.']],
['B21','Sicilian Defense','Smith-Morra Gambit','w','e4 c5 d4 cxd4 c3 dxc3 Nxc3 Nc6 Nf3 d6 Bc4 e6 O-O Nf6 Qe2 Be7 Rd1','White sacrifices a pawn to get fast development and open files against the Sicilian.',['Rooks on c1 and d1 hit Black\'s queenside and center.','Black must avoid traps (like the Siberian trap).','Black is a pawn up but must defend accurately.']],
['B25','Sicilian Defense','Closed Sicilian','w','e4 c5 Nc3 Nc6 g3 g6 Bg2 Bg7 d3 d6 Be3 e6 Qd2','White avoids the open center and plays for a kingside attack with f4-f5.',['White fianchettoes and keeps the position closed.','Black plays on the queenside with ...Rb8 and ...b5.','Spassky and Smyslov used it with success.']],
['B23','Sicilian Defense','Grand Prix Attack','w','e4 c5 Nc3 Nc6 f4 g6 Nf3 Bg7 Bb5 Nd4','An aggressive anti-Sicilian: f4 prepares a kingside attack with f5 and Qe1-h4.',['White targets the black king directly.','Black fights back with ...Nd4 and ...d5 ideas.','Popular in fast club tournaments.']],
['B31','Sicilian Defense','Rossolimo','w','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 g6 Bxc6 dxc6 d3 Bg7 h3','White pins the c6 knight and is happy to double Black\'s pawns.',['Avoids the open Sicilian.','White gives the bishop pair for structure.','Plans: Nc3, Be3, Qd2 and maybe Bh6.']],
['B52','Sicilian Defense','Moscow Variation','w','e4 c5 Nf3 d6 Bb5+ Bd7 Bxd7+ Qxd7 c4','A safe anti-Sicilian: White trades light-squared bishops and sets up a Maroczy-like clamp.',['Simple development for White, little theory.','Black\'s position is solid but less dynamic.','Good for players who want to avoid Najdorf theory.']],
['B29','Sicilian Defense','Nimzowitsch Variation','b','e4 c5 Nf3 Nf6 e5 Nd5 Nc3 e6 Nxd5 exd5 d4 Nc6','Black provokes e5 and fights against the advanced pawn.',['Black tries to undermine the e5 pawn.','Positions resemble the Alapin.','Rare and slightly risky.']],
// ---------------- French ----------------
['C18','French Defense','Winawer','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nc3 Bb4 e5 c5 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 Ne7 Qg4','Black pins the c3 knight and gives up the dark-squared bishop to damage White\'s pawns.',['White has the bishop pair and kingside chances (Qg4 hits g7).','Black has a better structure and queenside play.','One of the sharpest and most strategic openings.']],
['C14','French Defense','Classical, Steinitz Line','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Be7 e5 Nfd7 Bxe7 Qxe7 f4 O-O Nf3 c5','A classical setup: White gains space with e5 and f4; Black attacks the chain with ...c5 and ...f6.',['Attack the base of a pawn chain (d4).','White has kingside space; Black has queenside play.','The "bad" French bishop on c8 is Black\'s long-term problem.']],
['C11','French Defense','Steinitz Variation','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nc3 Nf6 e5 Nfd7 f4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 Be3','White builds the big center with e5 and f4; Black hits it with ...c5 and ...Nc6.',['The d4 pawn is Black\'s target.','White hopes for f5 breaks and kingside attack.','Black often plays ...Qb6 and ...cxd4.']],
['C12','French Defense','MacCutcheon','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Bb4 e5 h6 Bd2 Bxc3 bxc3 Ne4 Qg4','Black counter-pins the c3 knight, leading to sharp and unbalanced play.',['Black accepts kingside weaknesses for active pieces.','White\'s queen goes to g4 to hit g7.','Very tactical.']],
['C10','French Defense','Rubinstein','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Nd7 Nf3 Ngf6 Nxf6+ Nxf6','Black releases the central tension and aims for solid development.',['Black gives up the center but avoids weaknesses.','White has more space; Black hopes for ...c5 or ...b6 and ...Bb7.','A safe line to reduce theory.']],
['C07','French Defense','Tarrasch, Open (3...c5)','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nd2 c5 exd5 exd5 Ngf3 Nc6 Bb5 Bd6','Black accepts an isolated queen pawn in return for active pieces.',['An IQP gives Black open lines and outposts on e4 and c4.','White blockades on d4 and aims for an endgame.','Classic IQP strategy.']],
['C05','French Defense','Tarrasch, Closed (3...Nf6)','b','e4 e6 d4 d5 Nd2 Nf6 e5 Nfd7 Bd3 c5 c3 Nc6 Ne2','White gets a big pawn chain, Black undermines with ...c5 and ...f6.',['The d4 pawn is again Black\'s target.','White\'s pieces aim at the kingside.','Black must time ...f6 accurately.']],
['C02','French Defense','Advance Variation','w','e4 e6 d4 d5 e5 c5 c3 Nc6 Nf3 Qb6 a3 c4','White grabs space with e5; Black immediately attacks the base of the chain with ...c5.',['Attack the base of the pawn chain: Black targets d4.','...Qb6 hits b2 and d4.','White plans Bd3, O-O and kingside play.']],
['C01','French Defense','Exchange Variation','w','e4 e6 d4 d5 exd5 exd5 Bd3 Nc6 c3 Bd6','White exchanges in the center, creating a symmetrical structure. Black\'s light bishop is freed.',['Symmetry often leads to a quiet game.','Both sides fight for the e-file.','Black has no problem bishop in this line.']],
['C00','French Defense','King\'s Indian Attack setup','w','e4 e6 d3 d5 Nd2 Nf6 Ngf3 c5 g3 Nc6 Bg2 Be7 O-O O-O','White plays a reversed King\'s Indian setup against the French, aiming for e5 and a kingside attack.',['System opening: same setup against many defenses.','White pushes e5 and attacks with h4, Nf1-h2-g4.','Black plays on the queenside with ...b5-b4.']],
// ---------------- Caro-Kann ----------------
['B19','Caro-Kann Defense','Classical','b','e4 c6 d4 d5 Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Bf5 Ng3 Bg6 h4 h6 Nf3 Nd7 h5 Bh7 Bd3 Bxd3 Qxd3','Black develops the light-squared bishop outside the pawn chain before playing ...e6. Very solid.',['Black\'s structure has no weaknesses.','White gains space on the kingside with h4-h5.','Black castles queenside or stays flexible.']],
['B17','Caro-Kann Defense','Karpov (Steinitz) Variation','b','e4 c6 d4 d5 Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Nd7 Ng5 Ngf6 Bd3 e6 N1f3 Bd6 Qe2 h6 Ne4 Nxe4 Qxe4','Black prepares ...Ngf6 without allowing doubled pawns. Watch for the famous Nxe6 sacrifices.',['Be careful: 6...h6?? 7.Ne6! is a known trap.','Black aims for ...c5 or ...e5 breaks.','A world-championship-level defense.']],
['B16','Caro-Kann Defense','Bronstein-Larsen','b','e4 c6 d4 d5 Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Nf6 Nxf6+ gxf6','Black accepts doubled f-pawns for open g-file and active play.',['The g-file becomes a weapon for Black\'s rook.','Black\'s king may stay in the center or castle long.','Unbalanced and fighting.']],
['B12','Caro-Kann Defense','Advance Variation','w','e4 c6 d4 d5 e5 Bf5 Nf3 e6 Be2 c5 Be3','White gains space; Black develops the bishop outside the chain and attacks the base with ...c5.',['White\'s space vs Black\'s solid structure.','Black\'s ...c5 break is thematic.','White often castles and plays c3 or c4.']],
['B13','Caro-Kann Defense','Exchange Variation','w','e4 c6 d4 d5 exd5 cxd5 Bd3 Nc6 c3 Nf6 Bf4 Bg4 Qb3','A Carlsbad-like structure where White aims for kingside play and Black for the minority attack.',['Symmetric structure but White has easier development.','Qb3 hits b7.','Black should play ...Qd7 or ...Qc8.']],
['B14','Caro-Kann Defense','Panov-Botvinnik Attack','w','e4 c6 d4 d5 exd5 cxd5 c4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Nf3 Be7 cxd5 Nxd5','White plays c4, often leading to isolated queen pawn positions.',['White gets active pieces and the e5 outpost.','Black blockades d5 and aims for an endgame.','An IQP lesson from the white side.']],
['B11','Caro-Kann Defense','Two Knights','w','e4 c6 Nc3 d5 Nf3 Bg4 h3 Bxf3 Qxf3 e6','White develops knights first; Black gives up the bishop pair for a solid position.',['White has two bishops in an open-ish position.','Black has a solid pawn structure.','White plans d4 or d3 and g3.']],
['B12','Caro-Kann Defense','Fantasy Variation','w','e4 c6 d4 d5 f3 e6 Nc3 Bb4','White supports e4 with f3, keeping a big center.',['White wants to recapture on e4 with a pawn.','Black hits back with ...e6 and ...c5 or ...dxe4.','Sharp and offbeat.']],
// ---------------- Pirc / Modern / Alekhine / Scandinavian / others ----------------
['B08','Pirc Defense','Classical','b','e4 d6 d4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 Nf3 Bg7 Be2 O-O O-O c6','Black lets White build a center and plans to attack it later with ...e5 or ...c5.',['Hypermodern idea: control the center with pieces, then strike.','The g7 bishop is Black\'s best piece.','Black\'s breaks: ...e5, ...c5 and ...b5.']],
['B09','Pirc Defense','Austrian Attack','b','e4 d6 d4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 f4 Bg7 Nf3 O-O Bd3 Na6','White builds a giant center with f4 and threatens e5.',['White aims for e5 and a kingside attack.','Black must strike at the center with ...c5 or ...e5.','Dangerous for unprepared Black players.']],
['B07','Pirc Defense','150 Attack','b','e4 d6 d4 Nf6 Nc3 g6 Be3 Bg7 Qd2 c6 Bh6','White plays Be3, Qd2 and Bh6 to exchange Black\'s fianchettoed bishop and attack.',['White trades the g7 bishop and pushes h4-h5.','Black counterattacks on the queenside with ...b5.','Simple and effective for White.']],
['B06','Modern Defense','Main Line','b','e4 g6 d4 Bg7 Nc3 d6 Be3 a6 Qd2 b5','Black delays ...Nf6 and keeps great flexibility. Fianchetto first, decide later.',['Black can transpose to Pirc, KID or Sicilian structures.','...a6 and ...b5 gain queenside space.','Requires good understanding of pawn breaks.']],
['B03','Alekhine Defense','Four Pawns Attack','b','e4 Nf6 e5 Nd5 d4 d6 c4 Nb6 f4 dxe5 fxe5 Nc6 Be3 Bf5 Nc3 e6 Nf3','Black invites White to build a giant pawn center, then attacks it.',['Big centers can become targets.','Black pressures d4 and e5 with pieces.','If the center cracks, White\'s position collapses.']],
['B05','Alekhine Defense','Modern Variation','b','e4 Nf6 e5 Nd5 d4 d6 Nf3 Bg4 Be2 e6 O-O Be7 h3 Bh5 c4 Nb6','White develops calmly; Black pins the f3 knight and attacks the center.',['The e5 pawn is the key target.','Black plays ...dxe5 and ...Nc6 or ...Nd7.','Solid for both sides.']],
['B03','Alekhine Defense','Exchange Variation','b','e4 Nf6 e5 Nd5 d4 d6 c4 Nb6 exd6 cxd6 Nc3 g6','White trades on d6; Black gets a solid structure and a fianchetto.',['Black has an asymmetrical, flexible structure.','White has more space with c4 and d4.','Plans: ...Bg7, ...O-O and ...Nc6.']],
['B01','Scandinavian Defense','Main Line (3...Qa5)','b','e4 d5 exd5 Qxd5 Nc3 Qa5 d4 Nf6 Nf3 c6 Bc4 Bf5 Bd2 e6','Black challenges e4 immediately. The queen comes out early but finds a safe home on a5.',['Black gets quick development and a solid structure.','White gains time by attacking the queen.','The light bishop goes outside the chain before ...e6.']],
['B01','Scandinavian Defense','Modern (3...Qd6)','b','e4 d5 exd5 Qxd5 Nc3 Qd6 d4 Nf6 Nf3 a6 g3 Bg4 Bg2 Nc6','The queen retreats to d6, a modern and popular choice.',['On d6 the queen controls e5 and b4.','...a6 stops Nb5 tricks.','Black aims for ...O-O-O and active pieces.']],
['B01','Scandinavian Defense','2...Nf6 Modern','b','e4 d5 exd5 Nf6 d4 Nxd5 Nf3 g6 c4 Nb6','Black recaptures with the knight instead of the queen.',['Black avoids losing time with the queen.','White grabs space with c4.','Black plays ...Bg7 and attacks the center.']],
['B01','Scandinavian Defense','Portuguese Gambit','b','e4 d5 exd5 Nf6 d4 Bg4 f3 Bf5','A gambit: Black gives the d5 pawn for development and attacks.',['Black develops rapidly at the cost of a pawn.','White weakens the kingside with f3.','Tricky at fast time controls.']],
['B00','Owen Defense','Main Line','b','e4 b6 d4 Bb7 Bd3 e6 Nf3 c5 c3','Black fianchettoes the queen\'s bishop to pressure e4.',['The b7 bishop pressures e4.','Black plays ...c5 to hit the center.','Unusual but playable.']],
['B00','Nimzowitsch Defense','Kennedy-free Main Line','b','e4 Nc6 Nf3 d6 d4 Nf6 Nc3 Bg4','Black develops the knight first and pressures d4.',['Black pins f3 to pressure d4.','Offbeat but sound.','White has a small space edge.']],
// ---------------- 1.d4 d5 ----------------
['D63','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Orthodox Defense','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Be7 e3 O-O Nf3 Nbd7 Rc1 c6 Bd3 dxc4 Bxc4 Nd5','The classical QGD: Black holds the d5 point and develops solidly.',['White offers a pawn to deflect Black\'s d-pawn from the center.','Black\'s main problem is the c8 bishop.','Capablanca\'s freeing maneuver: ...dxc4 and ...Nd5.'],{3:'c4 — the Queen\'s Gambit.'}],
['D56','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Lasker Defense','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Be7 e3 O-O Nf3 h6 Bh4 Ne4 Bxe7 Qxe7','Black frees the position by trading minor pieces with ...Ne4.',['Exchanges relieve a cramped position.','Black follows with ...Nxc3 and ...dxc4/...e5.','Solid and drawish.']],
['D58','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Tartakower Defense','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Be7 e3 O-O Nf3 h6 Bh4 b6','Black fianchettoes the queen\'s bishop to solve the c8-bishop problem.',['The b7 bishop supports ...c5.','Hanging pawns (c5+d5) often arise.','A top-level main line for decades.']],
['D36','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Exchange, Carlsbad','w','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 cxd5 exd5 Bg5 c6 e3 Be7 Bd3 Nbd7 Qc2 O-O Nge2','White trades on d5 to fix Black\'s structure. Then the minority attack (b4-b5) or the e4 break.',['Minority attack: two pawns attack three to create a weakness on c6.','Black seeks kingside counterplay with ...Ne4 and ...f5.','A must-know structure.']],
['D38','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Ragozin','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Nf3 Bb4','A Nimzo-Indian style pin combined with ...d5.',['Black pins the c3 knight to fight for e4.','Sharp lines with Bg5 or Qa4+.','Active piece play.']],
['D34','Tarrasch Defense','Main Line','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 c5 cxd5 exd5 Nf3 Nc6 g3 Nf6 Bg2 Be7 O-O O-O','Black accepts an isolated d-pawn for free piece play.',['Black\'s IQP gives activity and space.','White blockades d4 and pressures d5 with Bg2.','Classic dynamic vs static fight.']],
['D41','Semi-Tarrasch Defense','Main Line','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Nf3 c5 cxd5 Nxd5 e4 Nxc3 bxc3 cxd4 cxd4 Bb4+','Black recaptures with the knight, leaving White a big pawn center.',['White has the c3/d4/e4 center; Black targets it.','Trades help Black.','The passed d-pawn can become strong for White.']],
['D52','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','Cambridge Springs','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Nbd7 e3 c6 Nf3 Qa5','Black uses the pin on the c3 knight and the unprotected g5 bishop.',['The queen on a5 pins c3 and eyes g5.','Watch for ...Ne4 and ...Bb4 tricks.','A trap-filled line.']],
['D37','Queen\'s Gambit Declined','5.Bf4','w','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Nf3 Be7 Bf4 O-O e3 c5','White develops the bishop to f4, keeping more control over e5 and c7.',['Modern main line, more flexible than Bg5.','Black hits back with ...c5.','IQP positions often arise.']],
['D27','Queen\'s Gambit Accepted','Classical','b','d4 d5 c4 dxc4 Nf3 Nf6 e3 e6 Bxc4 c5 O-O a6','Black takes the pawn but does not try to keep it; instead Black hits the center with ...c5.',['Never try to hold c4 with ...b5 — it loses to a4 and Qf3 ideas.','Black attacks d4 and plays ...b5 and ...Bb7 later.','White often gets an IQP.']],
['D20','Queen\'s Gambit Accepted','Central Variation 3.e4','w','d4 d5 c4 dxc4 e4 e5 Nf3 exd4 Bxc4 Nc6','White occupies the center with e4 immediately.',['White builds a big center.','Black counterattacks with ...e5.','Open, tactical play.']],
['D17','Slav Defense','Main Line','b','d4 d5 c4 c6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 dxc4 a4 Bf5 e3 e6 Bxc4 Bb4 O-O','Black supports d5 with ...c6 to keep the c8 bishop free.',['The bishop gets out to f5 before ...e6.','After ...dxc4 Black fights for ...b5.','Rock solid and popular at every level.']],
['D13','Slav Defense','Exchange Variation','w','d4 d5 c4 c6 cxd5 cxd5 Nc3 Nf6 Bf4 Nc6 e3','White trades on d5 to create a symmetrical structure.',['Symmetry, but White has the move.','The open c-file matters.','Often drawish, but good for learning patience.']],
['D47','Semi-Slav Defense','Meran Variation','b','d4 d5 c4 c6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 e3 Nbd7 Bd3 dxc4 Bxc4 b5 Bd3','Black combines ...c6 and ...e6, then takes on c4 and expands with ...b5.',['Black gains queenside space with tempo.','The c5 break is Black\'s main idea.','Rich and dynamic.']],
['D44','Semi-Slav Defense','Botvinnik Variation','b','d4 d5 c4 c6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Bg5 dxc4 e4 b5 e5 h6 Bh4 g5','One of the sharpest openings in chess: both sides create enormous imbalances.',['Black grabs the c4 pawn and keeps it with ...b5.','White sacrifices a piece in many lines.','Pure calculation.']],
['D43','Semi-Slav Defense','Moscow Variation','b','d4 d5 c4 c6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 Bg5 h6 Bxf6 Qxf6','Black gets the bishop pair in exchange for some time.',['Solid choice with the two bishops.','White has a space advantage.','Black\'s position is hard to crack.']],
['D15','Slav Defense','Chebanenko (4...a6)','b','d4 d5 c4 c6 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 a6','A flexible move preparing ...b5 and keeping options for the c8 bishop.',['Black prepares ...b5 to grab space.','Very flexible — hard for White to choose a plan.','Popular among elite players.']],
['D08','Albin Countergambit','Main Line','b','d4 d5 c4 e5 dxe5 d4 Nf3 Nc6 g3','Black sacrifices a pawn for a cramping pawn on d4.',['Watch out for the Lasker Trap (...Bb4+ and ...dxe3).','The d4 pawn disrupts White\'s development.','Black castles queenside and attacks.']],
['D07','Chigorin Defense','Main Line','b','d4 d5 c4 Nc6 Nf3 Bg4 cxd5 Bxf3 gxf3 Qxd5','Black develops pieces instead of supporting d5 with pawns.',['Black gives up the bishop pair for development.','Piece pressure on d4.','Original and unbalanced.']],
['D02','London System','Main Line vs ...d5','w','d4 d5 Bf4 Nf6 e3 e6 Nf3 c5 c3 Nc6 Nbd2 Bd6 Bg3 O-O Bd3','A system opening: White develops the same way against almost anything: Bf4, e3, c3, Nf3, Nbd2, Bd3.',['Easy to learn, solid and flexible.','Bg3 answers ...Bd6 to keep the bishop.','White often plays Ne5 and a kingside attack.']],
['A48','London System','vs King\'s Indian setup','w','d4 Nf6 Nf3 g6 Bf4 Bg7 e3 O-O Be2 d6 h3','The London against a kingside fianchetto: White keeps a solid setup.',['h3 keeps a retreat for the f4 bishop.','Black plays ...c5 or ...e5 breaks.','White plays c3 and O-O.']],
['D00','Jobava London','Main Line','w','d4 d5 Nc3 Nf6 Bf4 c5 e3 Nc6 Nb5','An aggressive London with Nc3 instead of c3, aiming for quick attacks on c7.',['Nb5 targets c7.','White can castle either side.','Fresh and fighting.']],
['D05','Colle System','Colle-Koltanowski','w','d4 d5 Nf3 Nf6 e3 e6 Bd3 c5 c3 Nc6 Nbd2 Bd6 O-O O-O dxc5 Bxc5 e4','White sets up quietly, then breaks with e4 to open lines for a kingside attack.',['The e4 break is the key idea.','The d3 bishop points at h7.','Easy to learn system for club players.']],
['D05','Colle System','Colle-Zukertort','w','d4 d5 Nf3 Nf6 e3 e6 Bd3 c5 b3 Nc6 O-O Bd6 Bb2 O-O Nbd2','White fianchettoes the queen\'s bishop and aims Ne5, f4 and a kingside attack.',['The b2 bishop and d3 bishop aim at Black\'s king.','Ne5 and f4 form a strong attacking setup.','A favorite of club attackers.']],
['D00','Stonewall Attack','Main Line','w','d4 d5 e3 Nf6 Bd3 c5 c3 Nc6 f4','White plants pawns on c3, d4, e3, f4 and attacks with Ne5, Qf3 and g4.',['A rigid but very aggressive setup.','The e5 square is White\'s outpost.','Black should play ...Bf5 or ...Bg4 early.']],
['A46','Torre Attack','Main Line','w','d4 Nf6 Nf3 e6 Bg5 c5 e3 h6 Bh4','White pins the f6 knight early.',['White plans c3, Nbd2 and e4.','Black hits back with ...c5 and ...Qb6.','Solid and low-theory.']],
['A45','Trompowsky Attack','2...Ne4','w','d4 Nf6 Bg5 Ne4 Bf4 c5 f3 Qa5+ c3 Nf6','White threatens to double Black\'s pawns with Bxf6 at move 2.',['Unusual structures appear.','Black often attacks b2 with ...Qb6 or ...Qa5.','Practical surprise weapon.']],
['D01','Richter-Veresov Attack','Main Line','w','d4 Nf6 Nc3 d5 Bg5 Nbd7 Nf3 g6','White develops the queen\'s knight in front of the c-pawn and pins f6.',['White aims for e4 in one go.','Black chooses between ...c5 and ...g6 setups.','Offbeat.']],
['D00','Blackmar-Diemer Gambit','Main Line','w','d4 d5 e4 dxe4 Nc3 Nf6 f3 exf3 Nxf3 Bg4 h3 Bxf3 Qxf3 c6','White gambits a pawn for rapid development and an open f-file.',['Attack along the f-file with Qf3, Bc4 and O-O.','Black is a pawn up and should play solidly.','Traps galore.']],
// ---------------- Indian defenses ----------------
['E97','King\'s Indian Defense','Classical, Mar del Plata','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 Bg7 e4 d6 Nf3 O-O Be2 e5 O-O Nc6 d5 Ne7 Ne1 Nd7 f3 f5','Black lets White build a big center and launches a kingside pawn storm with ...f5-f4 and ...g5.',['After d5 the center is closed: White attacks queenside, Black kingside.','Speed matters — race of pawn storms.','A favorite of Fischer and Kasparov.']],
['E81','King\'s Indian Defense','Sämisch','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 Bg7 e4 d6 f3 O-O Be3 e5 Nge2 c6 Qd2 Nbd7','White supports e4 with f3 and prepares queenside castling and a kingside pawn storm.',['f3 takes away ...Ng4.','White often castles long and plays h4-h5.','Black counters with ...c6, ...b5 or ...e5.']],
['E76','King\'s Indian Defense','Four Pawns Attack','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 Bg7 e4 d6 f4 O-O Nf3 c5 d5 e6 Be2 exd5 cxd5','White grabs maximum space with four center pawns.',['Big centers can be targets: ...c5 and ...e6 break it up.','Sharp play with e5 pushes.','Black\'s pieces become active if the center opens.']],
['E62','King\'s Indian Defense','Fianchetto Variation','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nf3 Bg7 g3 O-O Bg2 d6 O-O Nbd7 Nc3 e5 e4','White calmly fianchettoes, reducing Black\'s attacking chances.',['Solid and positional.','Black plays ...e5 or ...c5 and ...Qa5 ideas.','The center often stays tense.']],
['E73','King\'s Indian Defense','Averbakh','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 Bg7 e4 d6 Be2 O-O Bg5 c5 d5','White pins with Bg5 to prevent ...e5 and ...f5 ideas.',['White restrains Black\'s usual counterplay.','Black plays ...c5, ...e6 and ...h6.','Positional and subtle.']],
['D85','Grünfeld Defense','Exchange Variation','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 d5 cxd5 Nxd5 e4 Nxc3 bxc3 Bg7 Bc4 c5 Ne2 Nc6 Be3 O-O O-O','Black allows a big white center and then attacks it with pieces and ...c5.',['The g7 bishop and ...c5 pressure d4 relentlessly.','White aims for d5 or a kingside attack.','Kasparov\'s main weapon.']],
['D97','Grünfeld Defense','Russian System','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 d5 Nf3 Bg7 Qb3 dxc4 Qxc4 O-O e4 Bg4','White attacks d5 with the queen; Black gives up the center for piece play.',['White gets a big center; Black pressures it.','...Bg4 pressures d4 via f3.','Rich theory.']],
['D82','Grünfeld Defense','4.Bf4','b','d4 Nf6 c4 g6 Nc3 d5 Bf4 Bg7 e3 c5 dxc5 Qa5','White develops the bishop to f4 to pressure c7.',['Black sacrifices a pawn for activity.','Tactics on the a5-e1 diagonal.','Sharp.']],
['E54','Nimzo-Indian Defense','Rubinstein (4.e3)','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nc3 Bb4 e3 O-O Bd3 d5 Nf3 c5 O-O Nc6 a3 Bxc3 bxc3','Black pins the c3 knight to control e4. One of the most respected defenses.',['Black is ready to give the bishop for the knight to double White\'s pawns.','The fight is about e4.','Flexible and deep.']],
['E32','Nimzo-Indian Defense','Classical (4.Qc2)','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nc3 Bb4 Qc2 O-O a3 Bxc3+ Qxc3 b6 Bg5 Bb7','White avoids doubled pawns by recapturing with the queen.',['White keeps the bishop pair.','Black controls light squares with ...b6 and ...Bb7.','Strategic battle.']],
['E25','Nimzo-Indian Defense','Sämisch','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nc3 Bb4 a3 Bxc3+ bxc3 c5 f3 d5 cxd5 Nxd5','White forces the bishop for knight trade and plans e4.',['White has the bishop pair and center.','Black attacks the weak c4/c3 pawns.','Clear strategic themes.']],
['E15','Queen\'s Indian Defense','Main Line','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nf3 b6 g3 Ba6 b3 Bb4+ Bd2 Be7 Bg2 c6 Bc3 d5','Black fights for the e4 square with the bishop on b7/a6.',['Light-square control.','Black is solid and flexible.','White has a slight space edge.']],
['E12','Queen\'s Indian Defense','Petrosian Variation','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nf3 b6 a3 Bb7 Nc3 d5 cxd5 Nxd5','White prevents ...Bb4 with a3 and plans e4.',['a3 prophylaxis against ...Bb4.','White plans Qc2 and e4.','Black keeps a solid position.']],
['E11','Bogo-Indian Defense','Main Line','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 Nf3 Bb4+ Bd2 Qe7 g3 Nc6 Bg2 Bxd2+ Nbxd2 d6','Black checks on b4 to disrupt White\'s development.',['Black exchanges dark-squared bishops.','Plans with ...e5.','Solid and simple.']],
['E04','Catalan Opening','Open Variation','w','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 g3 d5 Bg2 dxc4 Nf3 Be7 O-O O-O Qc2 a6 Qxc4 b5 Qc2 Bb7','White fianchettoes the king\'s bishop to pressure the long diagonal and d5/b7.',['The g2 bishop is a monster on the long diagonal.','White wins back the c4 pawn.','Long-term positional pressure.']],
['E06','Catalan Opening','Closed Variation','w','d4 Nf6 c4 e6 g3 d5 Bg2 Be7 Nf3 O-O O-O c6 Qc2 Nbd7 Nbd2 b6 e4','Black keeps the center closed with ...c6; White breaks with e4.',['The e4 break opens the g2 bishop.','Black\'s c8 bishop goes to b7.','Positional subtlety.']],
['A70','Benoni Defense','Modern Main Line','b','d4 Nf6 c4 c5 d5 e6 Nc3 exd5 cxd5 d6 e4 g6 Nf3 Bg7 Be2 O-O O-O','Black creates an asymmetrical structure: White has a central majority, Black a queenside majority.',['Black plays ...Re8, ...Na6-c7 and ...b5.','White plays for e5.','Dynamic and risky.']],
['A57','Benko Gambit','Accepted','b','d4 Nf6 c4 c5 d5 b5 cxb5 a6 bxa6 Bxa6 Nc3 d6 e4 Bxf1 Kxf1 g6','Black sacrifices a pawn for long-term pressure on the a- and b-files.',['Open files on the queenside for Black\'s rooks.','The g7 bishop supports the queenside pressure.','Black is often happy even in the endgame.']],
['A54','Old Indian Defense','Main Line','b','d4 Nf6 c4 d6 Nc3 e5 Nf3 Nbd7 e4 Be7','Black sets up a compact position with ...d6 and ...e5, but the bishop goes to e7.',['Solid but passive.','Black keeps a strong point on e5.','A slower relative of the King\'s Indian.']],
['A52','Budapest Gambit','Main Line','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e5 dxe5 Ng4 Bf4 Nc6 Nf3 Bb4+','Black sacrifices the e-pawn and tries to win it back on e5.',['Black attacks e5 with pieces.','Watch the Kieninger trap.','Tricky and fun.']],
['A87','Dutch Defense','Leningrad','b','d4 f5 g3 Nf6 Bg2 g6 Nf3 Bg7 O-O O-O c4 d6 Nc3 Qe8','Black combines ...f5 with a kingside fianchetto, aiming for ...e5.',['Fight for e5 and e4.','Kingside space for Black.','Dynamic counterattacking defense.']],
['A90','Dutch Defense','Stonewall','b','d4 f5 c4 Nf6 g3 e6 Bg2 d5 Nf3 c6 O-O Bd6','Black builds a pawn wall on c6-d5-e6-f5 and controls e4.',['The e4 square is Black\'s outpost.','The e5 square is White\'s.','Black\'s light bishop is the problem piece.']],
['A96','Dutch Defense','Classical','b','d4 f5 c4 Nf6 g3 e6 Bg2 Be7 Nf3 O-O O-O d6','Flexible development with ...d6 and ...e5 ideas.',['Black aims for ...Qe8-h5 attack.','Central break ...e5.','Solid and fighting.']],
['A82','Dutch Defense','Staunton Gambit','w','d4 f5 e4 fxe4 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5','White gambits a pawn against the Dutch to open lines quickly.',['Exploits the weakened e8-h5 diagonal.','White regains e4 or gets attack.','Sharp.']],
['A40','Englund Gambit','Main Line','b','d4 e5 dxe5 Nc6 Nf3 Qe7','Black gives up the e-pawn hoping for traps. Objectively bad but dangerous for the unprepared.',['The main trap: Bf4? Qb4+! hitting b2.','White should play Qd5 or Nc3.','Shows why material greed can backfire.']],
// ---------------- Flank ----------------
['A38','English Opening','Symmetrical','w','c4 c5 Nc3 Nc6 g3 g6 Bg2 Bg7 Nf3 Nf6 O-O O-O','White starts with the c-pawn and fights for d5. Symmetrical lines lead to rich maneuvering.',['The g2 bishop pressures the long diagonal.','Plans: a3, Rb1 and b4 to expand.','Patient positional play.']],
['A29','English Opening','Reversed Sicilian','w','c4 e5 Nc3 Nf6 Nf3 Nc6 g3 d5 cxd5 Nxd5 Bg2 Nb6 O-O Be7','A Sicilian Dragon with colors reversed and an extra tempo for White.',['White uses the extra tempo to play on the queenside.','Black has a big center.','Understanding reversed openings is a powerful skill.']],
['A26','English Opening','Botvinnik System','w','c4 e5 Nc3 Nc6 g3 g6 Bg2 Bg7 e4 d6 Nge2','White sets up pawns on c4 and e4 for a firm grip on d5.',['Control the d5 square.','Plans: d3, O-O, f4.','Strategic and hard to crack.']],
['A18','English Opening','Mikenas-Carls','w','c4 Nf6 Nc3 e6 e4 d5 e5 d4 exf6 dxc3 bxc3 Qxf6 d4','A sharp line where both sides trade knights and pawns in the center.',['White gets a strong center.','Black has a lead in development.','Tactical.']],
['A30','English Opening','Hedgehog','b','c4 c5 Nf3 Nf6 Nc3 e6 g3 b6 Bg2 Bb7 O-O Be7 d4 cxd4 Qxd4 d6','Black sets up pawns on the 6th rank (a6, b6, d6, e6) like a hedgehog, waiting to spring with ...b5 or ...d5.',['Small space but no weaknesses.','Black\'s breaks: ...b5 and ...d5.','White must be careful not to overextend.']],
['A09','Réti Opening','Advance (2...d4)','w','Nf3 d5 c4 d4 e3 Nc6 exd4 Nxd4','A hypermodern opening: White attacks the center from the side.',['White challenges d4 with e3.','Black gets a central knight.','Flexible.']],
['A13','Réti Opening','Neo-Catalan','w','Nf3 d5 c4 e6 g3 Nf6 Bg2 Be7 O-O O-O b3','White develops with fianchettos and pressures the center from a distance.',['Double fianchetto possible with b3 and Bb2.','Black has a solid center.','Patient maneuvering.']],
['A07','King\'s Indian Attack','Main Line','w','Nf3 d5 g3 Nf6 Bg2 c6 O-O Bg4 d3 Nbd7 Nbd2 e5 e4','A reversed King\'s Indian: White sets up the same pieces almost regardless of Black\'s moves.',['Plans: e4-e5 and a kingside attack.','Low theory, high understanding.','Fischer used it with great success.']],
['A03','Bird\'s Opening','Main Line','w','f4 d5 Nf3 Nf6 e3 g6 Be2 Bg7 O-O O-O d3','White controls e5 with the f-pawn, a reversed Dutch.',['Control of e5 and kingside attack.','White often plays Qe1-h4.','Unusual but sound.']],
['A02','Bird\'s Opening','From\'s Gambit','b','f4 e5 fxe5 d6 exd6 Bxd6 Nf3 g5','Black sacrifices a pawn against the Bird to attack the weakened white king.',['Black aims for ...g4 and ...Bxh2.','White must play Nf3 and g3/d4 accurately.','Tactically dangerous.']],
['A01','Larsen\'s Opening','Main Line','w','b3 e5 Bb2 Nc6 e3 d5 Bb5 Bd6','White fianchettoes the queen\'s bishop at once to pressure e5.',['The b2 bishop pressures the long diagonal.','Black takes the center.','Hypermodern ideas.']],
['A00','Polish (Sokolsky) Opening','Main Line','w','b4 e5 Bb2 Bxb4 Bxe5 Nf6 c4','White grabs queenside space and pressures e5 with Bb2.',['Unusual but tricky.','White gives a pawn for a strong bishop.','Black should develop quickly.']],
['A00','Grob Opening','Main Line','w','g4 d5 Bg2 Bxg4 c4 c6','A provocative pawn move that weakens White\'s kingside.',['Objectively dubious.','White pressures d5 and b7.','Useful to know how to punish.']],
// ---------------- Traps (learn to set them and avoid them) ----------------
['TRP','Opening Trap','Fool\'s Mate','b','f3 e5 g4 Qh4#','The fastest possible checkmate. It shows how dangerous it is to weaken the e1-h4 diagonal.',['Never weaken the f- and g-pawns early.','The king needs pawns in front of it.','Mate in 2 moves!']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Scholar\'s Mate','w','e4 e5 Bc4 Nc6 Qh5 Nf6 Qxf7#','Queen and bishop attack f7, the weakest square near the black king.',['f7 is only defended by the king at the start.','Defend with ...g6 or ...Qe7, not ...Nf6.','Bringing the queen out early is risky against good defense.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Legal\'s Mate','w','e4 e5 Nf3 d6 Bc4 Bg4 Nc3 g6 Nxe5 Bxd1 Bxf7+ Ke7 Nd5#','White sacrifices the queen for a mate with three minor pieces.',['A pin isn\'t always a real pin: Nxe5! breaks it.','Greed (...Bxd1) is punished.','Minor pieces can mate together.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Blackburne Shilling Trap','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nd4 Nxe5 Qg5 Nxf7 Qxg2 Rf1 Qxe4+ Be2 Nf3#','Black sets a trap: if White grabs e5, Black\'s queen invades and mates.',['Taking material without checking threats can lose the game.','Smothered mate at the end.','Against ...Nd4, simply play Nxd4 or O-O.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Elephant Trap','b','d4 d5 c4 e6 Nc3 Nf6 Bg5 Nbd7 cxd5 exd5 Nxd5 Nxd5 Bxd8 Bb4+ Qd2 Bxd2+ Kxd2 Kxd8','In the QGD, the d5 pawn looks loose but it is a trap: the pinned knight is not really pinned.',['After Nxd5? Nxd5! Bxd8 Bb4+ Black wins a piece.','Check if a "pin" is real — the queen can be given if there is a counter-blow.','Very common at club level.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Lasker Trap','b','d4 d5 c4 e5 dxe5 d4 e3 Bb4+ Bd2 dxe3 Bxb4 exf2+ Ke2 fxg1=N+','In the Albin Countergambit, an underpromotion to a knight wins material.',['Underpromotion with check!','The pawn on e3 is a monster.','After Rxg1 Bg4+ wins the queen.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Englund Gambit Trap','b','d4 e5 dxe5 Nc6 Nf3 Qe7 Bf4 Qb4+ Bd2 Qxb2 Bc3 Bb4 Qd2 Bxc3 Qxc3 Qc1#','A famous trap: White grabs the b4 check bait and gets mated on c1.',['Bf4?? allows ...Qb4+ hitting b2.','Bc3?? walks into ...Bb4! and mate.','Always check what your opponent threatens.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Siberian Trap','b','e4 c5 d4 cxd4 c3 dxc3 Nxc3 Nc6 Nf3 e6 Bc4 Qc7 O-O Nf6 Qe2 Ng4 h3 Nd4 Nxd4 Qh2#','In the Smith-Morra, Black\'s queen and knight team up for mate on h2.',['h3 seems to win the knight, but ...Nd4! deflects the queen.','The queen on c7 was aiming at h2 all along.','Look for long diagonals!']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Kieninger Trap','b','d4 Nf6 c4 e5 dxe5 Ng4 Nf3 Nc6 Bf4 Bb4+ Nbd2 Qe7 a3 Ngxe5 axb4 Nd3#','In the Budapest Gambit, a smothered mate on d3 punishes the greedy axb4.',['The king on e1 is boxed in by its own pieces.','Smothered mate patterns appear in many openings.','Before winning a piece, check for mates.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Stafford Gambit Trap','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nf6 Nxe5 Nc6 Nxc6 dxc6 d3 Bc5 Bg5 Nxe4 Bxd8 Bxf2+ Ke2 Bg4#','White pins the knight and grabs the queen but gets mated.',['Bg5?? looks natural but walks into ...Nxe4!','Bishops on c5 and g4 combine for mate.','A wonderful mating net.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Caro-Kann Smothered Mate','w','e4 c6 d4 d5 Nc3 dxe4 Nxe4 Nd7 Qe2 Ngf6 Nd6#','In the Caro-Kann, the e7 pawn is pinned by the queen: Nd6 is mate!',['A pinned pawn cannot capture.','Smothered mate: the king is boxed in by its own pieces.','Black should play 5...Ndf6 first.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Petrov Discovered Check','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nf6 Nxe5 Nxe4 Qe2 Nf6 Nc6+','Copying White\'s moves in the Petrov loses the queen to a discovered check.',['After Qe2, the e-file is opened toward the black king.','Discovered check: the knight moves and the queen gives check.','Learn the correct order: 3...d6 first.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Magnus Smith Trap','w','e4 c5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 cxd4 Nxd4 Nf6 Nc3 d6 Bc4 g6 Nxc6 bxc6 e5 dxe5 Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8','In the Sicilian, opening the d-file lets White win the queen with a bishop sacrifice on f7.',['Bxf7+ deflects the king, then Qxd8.','Black should play ...Ng4 or ...Nd5 instead of ...dxe5.','Watch open files toward your queen.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Noah\'s Ark Trap','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 d6 d4 b5 Bb3 Nxd4 Nxd4 exd4 Qxd4 c5 Qd5 Be6 Qc6+ Bd7 Qd5 c4','In the Ruy Lopez, the white bishop on b3 gets trapped by Black\'s pawns.',['Pawns can trap pieces: ...c5 and ...c4.','Qxd4 was a mistake.','Remember the "ark" of pawns on a6-b5-c4.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Fishing Pole Trap','b','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 Nf6 O-O Ng4 h3 h5 hxg4 hxg4 Nxe5 Qh4','Black offers the knight on g4. Taking it opens the h-file for a mating attack.',['The h-file opens toward the white king.','Threat: ...Qh1# or ...Qh2#.','Don\'t take "fish" without checking the hook.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Queen\'s Gambit Accepted Greed','w','d4 d5 c4 dxc4 e3 b5 a4 c6 axb5 cxb5 Qf3','Black tries to keep the c4 pawn with ...b5, but the a8 rook falls.',['Holding the c4 pawn with ...b5 doesn\'t work.','Qf3 forks the a8 rook along the diagonal.','Return gambit pawns when keeping them costs too much.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Tarrasch Trap','w','e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Nxe4 d4 b5 Bb3 d5 dxe5 Be6 c3 Be7 Re1 O-O Nd4 Qd7 Nxe6 fxe6 Rxe4','In the Open Ruy Lopez, the e4 knight is pinned against... nothing? Watch the e-file!',['After Nxe6 fxe6, the d5 pawn is pinned to the e6 pawn and cannot protect e4.','Overloaded defenders lose material.','Rxe4 wins a piece.']],
['TRP','Opening Trap','Damiano Defense Trap','w','e4 e5 Nf3 f6 Nxe5 fxe5 Qh5+ Ke7 Qxe5+ Kf7 Bc4+ d5 Bxd5+ Kg6 h4 h5 Bxb7 Bxb7 Qf5+ Kh6 d4+ g5 Qf7','A long forced sequence: Black\'s king is hunted after ...f6.',['The king hunt shows why ...f6 is so weakening.','Every white move is a check or a big threat.','Threat: Qg6# or hxg5#.']]
];
